  "slug": "visual-activity-hierarchical-2c1b",
  "summary": "Quantitative benchmarking of computational models against behavioral and neural recordings is now central across recent preprints and a published study.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is explicit computational models evaluated quantitatively against neural or behavioral data. A sensory-realistic network matches human working-memory behavior more closely than abstract-input models, with the gain attributed mainly to input realism. NeuroHorizon forecasts macaque and mouse neural population activity up to 1 s ahead using hierarchical prediction memory and scheduled sampling. FDTest applies a generalized fluctuation-dissipation test to quantify local memory effects in whole-brain models. A tutorial on neural simulation-based inference addresses cognitive models with intractable likelihoods, and AMRT offers a shared two-stage decoder that generalizes continuous motor decoding across subjects. What is new is the emphasis on biologically grounded inputs, memory structure, and quantitative comparison, though the scope across these works is heterogeneous.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-17"
}
